From faacebcf4c5017bcff9c9caf13dff61e6f45ab18 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StonkGuy <68372320+StonkGuy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 01:52:21 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/9] LinuxSyscalls: emulate x86-64 ptrace for tracees that
 also run under FEX

Injector-style launchers (the Easy Anti-Cheat Linux launcher that Proton runs is the motivating case) drive their
target with PTRACE_TRACEME / PTRACE_SYSCALL / GETREGSET / SETREGSET / POKE and expect the x86-64 view of it. When
tracer and tracee are both FEX guests the host kernel only sees aarch64 FEX processes, so none of that was true.

PtraceEmu keeps a small state block in the tracee (a shared file-backed page for a PTRACE_TRACEME child, the image's
static block after its exec) that the tracer reads and writes through ptrace PEEK/POKE. The tracee reports syscall
entry/exit and guest int3/SIGSEGV as stops by raising a host SIGSTOP that the tracer's wait4() wrapper turns into
SIGTRAP|0x80 or the requested signal. Register writes are applied when the tracee resumes, memory POKEs invalidate
translated code, /proc/<pid>/exe of an exec()ed tracee reads back the guest binary, and PTRACE_DETACH switches the
emulation off.

As on Linux, orig_rax selects the syscall that runs after an entry stop; rax there is the -ENOSYS placeholder, so an
unmodified GETREGS/SETREGS (or GETREGSET/SETREGSET) round trip leaves the syscall unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
